From: Gianluca Mazza <info@gianlucamazza.it>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: spm: Report SAW voltage change timeouts

smp_set_vdd_v1_1() gives up after 200 us if SAW STS1 does not reach the
requested level, but spm_set_voltage_sel() still returns 0. The OPP core
then raises the CPU clock as if the new voltage had been reached. The
downstream msm_spm_set_vdd() returns -EIO in that case and acpuclock aborts
the frequency increase. Propagate -ETIMEDOUT and keep the old selector.

RISKY: if the STS1 comparison is wrong on some SoC, every voltage change
will now fail (the existing rate-limited error would already show that).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c
@@ -85,6 +85,7 @@
 	struct device *dev;
 	unsigned int volt_sel;
 	int reg_cpu;
+	int vdd_err;
 };
 
 static const u16 spm_reg_offset_v4_1[SPM_REG_NR] = {
@@ -321,11 +322,25 @@
 static int spm_set_voltage_sel(struct regulator_dev *rdev, unsigned int selector)
 {
 	struct spm_driver_data *drv = rdev_get_drvdata(rdev);
+	unsigned int old_sel = drv->volt_sel;
+	int ret;
 
 	drv->volt_sel = selector;
+	drv->vdd_err = 0;
 
 	/* Always do the SAW register writes on the corresponding CPU */
-	return smp_call_function_single(drv->reg_cpu, drv->reg_data->set_vdd, drv, true);
+	ret = smp_call_function_single(drv->reg_cpu, drv->reg_data->set_vdd, drv, true);
+	if (!ret)
+		ret = drv->vdd_err;
+	if (ret)
+		/*
+		 * Report the failure so that the OPP core does not go on to
+		 * raise the CPU frequency on a rail that may not have reached
+		 * the new level. The downstream driver returns -EIO here too.
+		 */
+		drv->volt_sel = old_sel;
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int spm_get_voltage_sel(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
@@ -382,6 +397,7 @@
 				     1, 200, false,
 				     drv, SPM_REG_STS1)) {
 		dev_err_ratelimited(drv->dev, "timeout setting the voltage (%x %x)!\n", sts, vlevel);
+		drv->vdd_err = -ETIMEDOUT;
 		goto enable_avs;
 	}
 
